# Director Appointment — System Context

This project implements the **Director Appointment** workflow for a Corporate
Secretary persona (CoSec at Acme, Inc.), end-to-end across UI, chat, and
deterministic backend channels. Before designing components, data shapes,
state, or business logic, consult the canonical specs in `docs/`.

## Reference Documents (read on demand)

- `docs/01-product-and-persona.md` — what the workflow does, who it serves,
  channel-agnostic operating principles, success criteria.
- `docs/02-mock-data.md` — canonical reference data: parent org, subsidiary
  entities, directors, HR candidate pool, regulator/jurisdiction fixtures,
  field shapes to preserve when swapping for live integrations.
- `docs/03-user-journey.md` — the six logical workflow steps (trigger →
  identify → collect → configure → approve → file/close), with explicit
  agent / human / system responsibilities and inputs/outputs per step.
- `docs/04-workflow-engine.md` — runtime model: `AppointmentWorkflow` state
  shape, state machine, autonomous-segment orchestration, integrations
  (HRIS, entity register, regulator e-filing), timing.
- `docs/05-business-rules.md` — invariants and edge cases: jurisdiction
  rules (SG/ACRA, US-DE, IE, NL), eligibility, validation, approval
  routing, filing requirements. These are MUST-enforce rules.

## How to Use

- Treat these documents as the source of truth for domain modeling,
  terminology, state shapes, and business rules. Match field names
  (e.g. `AppointmentWorkflow`, `EntityRef`, `Trigger`) exactly.
- The implementation is **channel-agnostic**: the same state and rules back
  the UI, chat, and backend. Don't bake channel-specific assumptions into
  shared modules.
- When a question touches jurisdiction, eligibility, consent forms, or
  filing, defer to `05-business-rules.md` — those rules are normative.
- If specs and code disagree, surface the conflict rather than silently
  diverging from the docs.
